Let's find this limit directly.

To do that, we will want to divide both the numerator and the denominator by the same quantity, in a way that will help us derive the limit.

Since the leading term of the numerator is $x$, let's divide by $x$.

In the denominator, let's divide by $-\sqrt{x^2}$, since for negative values, $x=-\sqrt{x^2}$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{3x}{\sqrt{16x^2-9x}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{3x}{x}}{\dfrac{\sqrt{16x^2-9x}}{-\sqrt{x^2}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide sides by }x=-\sqrt{x^2}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}-\dfrac{\dfrac{3x}{x}}{\dfrac{\sqrt{16x^2-9x}}{\sqrt{x^2}}}
\end{align}$

Now let's continue by simplifying the expression and using the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}-\dfrac{\dfrac{3\cancel{x}}{\cancel{x}}}{\sqrt{\dfrac{16\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}}-\dfrac{9\cancel x}{\cancel x \cdot x}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}-\dfrac{3}{\sqrt{16-\dfrac{9}{x}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}-\dfrac{3}{\sqrt{16-0}}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{3}{\sqrt{16}}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{3}{4}
\end{align}$
